WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.779
The late Sir Sean Connery will no doubt be
remembered first and foremost for being the

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:08.340
man who originated the role of James Bond
on the big screen.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.660
But prior to his retirement from acting in
2006, he starred in numerous iconic action

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.660
films.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.280
Aside from the James Bond movies, Connery
also appeared in Indiana Jones and the Last

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.830
Crusade, Highlander, and The Rock - all of
which include explosive scenes.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:28.470
That seems totally natural since they're all
action movies, but in a 2009 Total Film interview,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.730
comic book writer Alan Moore alleged that
some of the scenes featuring explosions in

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.990
Connery projects were actually a result of
a bizarre clause in the actor's contract.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:41.130
In his rant-filled interview, Moore discussed
The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen, which

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.370
was based on his comics of the same name.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:48.040
The movie, directed by Stephen Norrington,
was Connery's final live-action project before

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.770
he retired from acting in 2006, and he went
on record saying that the filming process

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.210
left him disenchanted with modern Hollywood.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.040
“I’m waiting to be impressed”

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.300
However, Moore blamed part of the movie's
troubles on the alleged demands of its star.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.300
He claimed,

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.400
"The film cost £100 million because Sean
Connery wanted £17 million of that - and

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.400
a bigger explosion than the one he'd had in
his last film.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.360
It's in his contract that he has to have a
bigger explosion with every film he's in.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.061
In The Rock, he'd blown up an island, and
he was demanding for our film that he blow

00:01:18.061 --> 00:01:19.860
up Venice or something like that.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.620
It would have been the moon in his next movie."

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:26.240
“Well, you don't come any closer than that!”

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.200
It should be noted that Moore is famously
cantankerous about Hollywood (and the comic

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.700
book industry), and if Connery was on a quest
to have a huge explosion in every single one

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.740
of his movies, it's never been documented
publicly by anyone else.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.460
That doesn't mean the clause never existed,
but there's a possibility that Moore was being

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.840
a tad hyperbolic.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.560
What is true is that the famed 007 actor and
Norrington clashed on the set of The League

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.310
of Extraordinary Gentlemen.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:55.090
Back when the movie was still in production
in 2002, an article in Entertainment Weekly

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.900
discussed the tensions on set, which included
Connery and Norrington allegedly nearly coming

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.080
to blows over an elephant gun.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.020
Connery said at the time,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.020
"Oh, yes, it's been difficult.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.260
Very, very difficult.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.780
There's no question about it."

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.740
In 2005, Connery opened up a bit more, telling
the BBC that his experience on the set of

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.340
The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen put
him off Hollywood for good.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.340
He said,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.650
"I'm fed up with the idiots ... the ever-widening
gap between people who know how to make movies

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.500
and the people who green-light the movies.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.849
I don't say they're all idiots.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:27.650
I'm just saying there's a lot of them that
are very good at it."

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:32.760
While he didn't explicitly call out Norrington
in that interview, in 2007, Connery did say

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.400
that the director "would have been arrested
for insanity," according to The Hollywood

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.400
Reporter.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.650
The actor also admitted that he attempted
to wrest editorial control away from the young

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.200
director in hopes of salvaging the movie.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.530
Connery's efforts didn't pan out, though,
and the movie ended up being a box office

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:48.680
flop anyway.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.240
Ultimately, Moore's claim that Connery craved
big-budget explosions in his films is just

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.069
one more strange footnote in the history of
a movie with backstage drama so legendary

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:00.019
that it truly deserves a film of its own.

